WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.040
Evidence review is where assessment quality is won or lost.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:13.220
Proxy logs, scanner output, screenshots, HTTP requests and responses, error messages, and behavior notes can all support findings.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:21.760
AI can summarize and categorize this material, but the analyst must verify that the summary matches the actual evidence and the scope of the engagement.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.380
Scanner output requires careful review.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:31.880
Automated tools can produce false positives, miss context, duplicate issues, or assign severity without understanding the application.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.620
AI summaries can inherit those weaknesses or add new ones.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:43.840
A better workflow is to ask AI to organize output by theme, identify items requiring manual confirmation, and highlight missing evidence.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.380
The final determination stays with the assessor.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.440
HTTP evidence can be especially useful because it records what the application actually sent and received.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:01:00.660
Requests, responses, status codes, headers, parameters, cookies, redirects, and response bodies may all matter.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:07.040
AI can help explain patterns or draft a plain-language summary, but sensitive values should be minimized and protected.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.060
If tokens, credentials, or personal data appear, handle them according to policy.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.560
Screenshots and behavior notes help tell the story when they are tied to timestamps and source references.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:26.020
The strongest findings usually combine clear evidence, application context, affected area, impact reasoning, and remediation guidance.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.760
AI can help structure that package, but every conclusion should be traceable back to verified observations.
